        {
            "number": 7,
            "revision": "2c016255e3973cc5aa3d7441ebb593fd5f50975c",
            "parents": [
                "450d6722a61a62877b70f4b508e20455f91eeffd"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/90/89190/7",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                "username": "jagpalgill"
            },
            "createdOn": 1776364184,
            "author": {
                "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                "username": "jagpalgill"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "rtu/device/base_device.cpp",
                    "line": 208,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": "This will end up reading maxSpanSize number of registers unconditionally right? Since no where do we change the size of readBuffer. Since readHoldingRegisters does not take in a size argument."
                },
                {
                    "file": "rtu/device/base_device.cpp",
                    "line": 208,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": "modbus spec is pretty explict here: https://www.modbus.org/file/secure/modbusprotocolspecification.pdf\n\n```\nThe data address received in the query is not an\nallowable address for the server. More\nspecifically, the combination of reference number\nand transfer length is invalid. For a controller with\n100 registers, the PDU addresses the first register\nas 0, and the last one as 99. If a request is\nsubmitted with a starting register address of 96\nand a quantity of registers of 4, then this request\nwill successfully operate (address-wise at least)\non registers 96, 97, 98, 99. If a request is\nsubmitted with a starting register address of 96\nand a quantity of registers of 5, then this request\nwill fail with Exception Code 0x02 \u201cIllegal Data\nAddress\u201d since it attempts to operate on registers\n96, 97, 98, 99 and 100, and there is no register\nwith address 100.\n```\nSo, any read request which involves part of it not being in the defined range of register addresses, the whole read-holding-registers request can return the 02 (ILLEGAL_DATA_ADDRESS) exception response."
                },
                {
                    "file": "rtu/device/base_device.cpp",
                    "line": 208,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": "It might really help if you convert readHoldingRegisters() to take in a std::span<uint16_t> instead of a std::vector<uint16_t>."
                },
                {
                    "file": "rtu/device/base_device.cpp",
                    "line": 208,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": "We might need a test for this as well. To make sure we always return 02 if the read-request involves reading illegal register addresses (since this passed the unit test when it should not)"
                },
                {
                    "file": "rtu/device/base_device.cpp",
                    "line": 208,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks for catching this. The ideal solution for this would be to use span for readHoldingRegister and other APIs, but that will be a larger change so will handle that as a followup patch. For now i have reverted this to use local readBuffer as before.\n\nWith this change reverted, currently all the registers that we are reading are valid one's so ideally we shouldn't hit this, but i have added a modbus test in this patch which tries to read an illegal data address and verifies it works as expected along with a related sensor tests as separate patch in https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/phosphor-modbus/+/89539."
                },
                {
                    "file": "rtu/device/base_device.cpp",
                    "line": 228,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": "[perf-nit] this will end up allocating. Can we use std::span(begin(), end()) instead? That would avoid the allocation. Buut then getRawIntegerFromRegister needs to be modified to take in a span. I suspect we can auto-convert from vector -> span but not the other way around."
                },
                {
                    "file": "rtu/device/base_device.cpp",
                    "line": 228,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "Done, i have changed getRawIntegerFromRegister to use span."
                }
            ],
